[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering Error Handling in Solidity: require, revert, assert, and Custom Errors"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Error handling is a critical aspect of smart contract development. It ensures that state changes are reverted when conditions are not met, saving gas and maintaining contract security. Solidity provides four primary mechanisms for error handling: require, revert, assert, and Custom Errors."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. The require Statement"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The require function is used to validate inputs and conditions before executing the main logic. If the condition evaluates to false, execution is aborted, state changes are reverted, and any remaining gas is refunded to the caller."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"function withdraw(uint256 amount) public {
    require(amount <= balances[msg.sender], "Insufficient balance");
    balances[msg.sender] -= amount;
    payable(msg.sender).transfer(amount);
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. The revert Statement"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"The revert function is useful when handling complex conditional branching. Instead of putting a long condition inside a require statement, you can use an if statement and trigger a revert explicitly."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"function transfer(address recipient, uint256 amount) public {
    if (recipient == address(0)) {
        revert("Invalid recipient address");
    }
    balances[msg.sender] -= amount;
    balances[recipient] += amount;
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"3. The assert Statement"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"The assert function should only be used to test for internal errors and to check invariants. Failing an assert condition consumes all remaining gas and indicates a bug in your contract logic."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"uint256 public totalSupply;

function updateTotalSupply(uint256 newSupply) public {
    totalSupply = newSupply;
    assert(totalSupply >= newSupply);
}"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Custom Errors (Gas Efficient)"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Introduced in Solidity 0.8.4, Custom Errors are a much more gas-efficient way to handle errors compared to string error messages. They allow you to pass parameters and are ABI-encoded."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"error InsufficientBalance(uint256 requested, uint256 available);

function withdrawCustom(uint256 amount) public {
    if (amount > balances[msg.sender]) {
        revert InsufficientBalance(amount, balances[msg.sender]);
    }
    balances[msg.sender] -= amount;
    payable(msg.sender).transfer(amount);
}"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Summary of Best Practices"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use require for input validation, access control, and checking external state conditions."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use revert with Custom Errors for complex logic and gas optimization."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use assert strictly for catching arithmetic overflows/underflows (pre-0.8) and checking critical contract invariants that should never be false."}]
